# EVERY PATTERN IN THIS FILE IS ANCHORED TO THE CONTEXT ROOT UNLESS IT STARTS
# WITH `**/`. That is Docker's grammar, not git's, and one line below --
# `data/` -- depends on it. Everything else here is written `**/` on purpose,
# because reading Docker's grammar as git's is what went wrong:
#
#   `__pycache__/` and `*.py[cod]` looked like "no bytecode anywhere" and meant
#   "no bytecode in ./". `COPY secure_context_pipeline/ secure_context_pipeline/`
#   copies an allow-listed directory *whole*, so every nested `__pycache__` the
#   developer's working tree happened to hold rode in with it: 38 `.pyc` files in
#   the tagged `secure-context-pipeline:1.0.0`, 65 in a probe build of the tree
#   as it stands. They were not inert. `python -v -c 'import
#   secure_context_pipeline.config'` in that image prints `code object from
#   '/app/secure_context_pipeline/__pycache__/config.cpython-311.pyc'`, so the
#   container ran bytecode compiled on a macOS host instead of bytecode derived
#   from the source it shipped. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` in the Dockerfile
#   cannot help: it stops the image from *writing* .pyc files, and these arrive
#   already written.
#
# The same anchoring made the secrets block inert below the root, which is the
# half that would have hurt: a probe context with a seeded
# `secure_context_pipeline/.env` and `secure_context_pipeline/crypto/tls.key`,
# built with this repository's own `COPY secure_context_pipeline/` line, shipped
# both -- while the root `.env` was correctly excluded. So the allow-list is not
# a reason to be relaxed here; it narrows the doorway, it does not close it.
#
# The other job of this file is size: everything below is otherwise uploaded to
# the daemon as build context on every build (the host virtualenv alone is
# ~750 MB), and it keeps a future `COPY . .` from silently re-introducing a
# secret at any depth.

# secrets -- must never reach a layer, a build cache, or a registry
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key

# runtime data: encrypted blobs, the session vault, the audit chain.
#
# `data/` is the one rule here that is anchored on purpose, and it is NOT the
# same rule as `data/` in `.gitignore` even though the two lines are identical.
# Docker anchors an un-`**`-ed pattern to the context root, so this line removes
# `./data` and nothing else. Git's grammar makes the identical line match a
# directory called `data` at ANY depth -- including
# `secure_context_pipeline/detection/data/`, where the gazetteers
# `entities.yaml` requires live, and `load_registry` refuses to start without
# them. Do not "harmonise" the two files, and do not sweep this line up with the
# `**/` conversion above: `**/data/` here ships an image whose container
# boot-fails on registry validation. Pinned by
# `test_every_runtime_asset_the_registry_needs_actually_reaches_the_image`, whose
# expectations were measured against a real build rather than reasoned about.
data/
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm

# host virtualenv -- wrong platform, and ~750 MB of build context
**/.venv/
**/venv/

# vcs + agent/editor state. `.DS_Store` is `**/` for a reason of its own: Finder
# writes one into every directory it opens, not just the root.
**/.git/
**/.gitignore
**/.claude/
# `.gstack/` was in `.gitignore` and not here, which is the wrong half to have. Git
# ignoring it means it is UNTRACKED, and the `.gitignore` block above says in its own
# words that untracked agent tooling is one careless `git add -A` from being shipped
# -- but the build context is uploaded from the WORKING TREE, where untracked and
# tracked look identical. So the file that governs what the daemon sees is exactly the
# one where an agent directory must not be missing. Swept the two files against each
# other line by line: this was the only pattern present in one and absent from the
# other. Nothing else on disk is agent or editor state (`.claude`, `.codegraph`,
# `.hypothesis`, `.DS_Store` are all already covered in both).
**/.gstack/
# 49 MB of code index and 648 KB of Hypothesis state. The image is unaffected -- the
# Dockerfile COPYs named paths, never `COPY . .` -- but every build ships them to the
# daemon as context, and they become a real leak the day someone widens a COPY line.
# `.codegraph` holds verbatim source extracted from this tree.
**/.codegraph/
**/.hypothesis/
**/.DS_Store

# python build artefacts and caches
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info/
**/build/
**/dist/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov/

# demo artefacts
**/wire_dump*.txt
